  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I'm halfway through my second term, which is really unbelievable. The last three years have absolutely flown by. Just prior to running for Congress, I served for six years in the state legislature during which time I also joined the Navy Reserves and served as a supply core officer, which is the logistics or what we like to think of as the business part of the Navy. And during that time, I deployed to Afghanistan 2014 and 15. On my way back from Afghanistan, my member of Congress decided to run for the Senate, and that opened up the seat. So I came home to an open seat situation, immediately became a candidate, ran for office, and became the most recently deployed member of Congress in the process. So a very fresh experience just having come home from Afghanistan going through the the mobilization and demobilization process as a reservist and having a unique background with that fresh experience. I come from northeast Indiana. We don't have an active duty military installation. We have a lot of business, a lot of defense related industry, although it's a fraction of the size that it was, pre sequestration, budget control act. We're trying to do something about that right now, but I didn't really have a compelling case to make when I got to Congress to serve on the Armed Services Committee because no one from my district had ever served on the Armed Services Committee before. But I went to Speaker Ryan at the time and others in Republican leadership and said, one, I have a passion for these issues. I just got home from Afghanistan of a unique experience and would really like to serve on that committee. And after a hard fought tussle with other, it was the most requested committee that year coming in in that 2016, after the 2016 election, it was the most requested committee. So felt very lucky to earn the support of Republican leadership to receive one of a few spots that were open.",
