  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, it seems like ancient history three years ago. But remember at the time, we went from Obama-era defense budgets to Trump-era defense budgets. So I had a front row seat in the first year, really the length of my first term, and seeing what type of leadership it took to advance the type of defense budgets that we need to get our military back on its feet and to make up for, at that point, eight to 10 years of defense cuts that brought our military to its knees to where it was pre-Trump. I believe that the most important victory of the Trump administration and of Congress during that time is restoring defense budgets to where they are today. So I got to see what that look like, what that type of leadership look like. And really it's not a, when it comes to defense budgets, let me make this very clear. It's not Republicans versus Democrats. It's defense minded members of Congress from both parties. working to convince our colleagues who aren't as defense minded, why this is important, why readiness has fallen behind because of defense cuts, post budget control act sequestration. So I believe, I mean, you want oftentimes as a member of Congress, you sit around and think, is this really a fulfilling experience? Is this really productive, especially these days? But looking back at those three years, there's not a doubt in my mind that we've made a difference when it comes to restoring defense budgets to over $700 billion baseline. I mean, now we're about to pass a NDAA that will authorize $750 billion baseline. That's something that I'm very proud of, having served, getting to Congress and being a part of in my early days.",
