    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "fact, people at home have a skewed view of Washington, D.C. from the 24-hour news cycle of the bickering back and forth between parties. I have great relationships with Democrat members who I work with on the Armed Services Committee, on the Veterans Committee, and across the board. I think you find that more the reality more in Congress is that we really do get along. We really do all have a vision and mission in mind of advancing our country. But my best friends in Congress are members who also served, who also wore the uniform, and many of them happen to be Democrats as well. So we serve, we go into the Armed Services Committee, we go on the Veterans Committee as well. We work hard on these issues together, we introduce bipartisan efforts When it comes to the Armed Services Committee, that's always been a bastion of bipartisanship. But you're absolutely right. Those members, I can think of, if I start naming off names, I'll forget some, but Jimmy Panetta and Seth Molten, who at some point, I'm sure we'll talk about our future of defense task force that he and I are co-charing and leading, Ruben Gallego and Andy Kim, a lot of members across the board who are Democrats that I treasure those friendships and relationships with on the other side of the aisle.",
