  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "I'm really excited about this effort. I mean, often in Washington and Congress, especially, we think in one year cycles, right? I mean, in fiscal year cycles, these days when it comes to CRs, we're thinking even less than a one year period. of time. But what Seth and I have been tasked with from both Chairman Adam Smith and the Armed Services Committee ranking member Thornberry is to set up a task force, which is a unique animal within the rules of the Congress. I'll come back to that in a moment. The mission of the future of defense task force is to not just think about the next year, but think about the next 10, 20, 30 years, how we can foster the type of innovation in our national defense to prepare for the threats of the future. What we're finding is that there are a lot of political interests, a lot of sacred cows that are protected. and authorized on the Armed Services Committee. And sometimes we need to really take an honest look at what platforms, what technologies maybe are outdated that we're authorizing today that prevent us from authorizing the type of innovation and fostering the innovation base that will allow us to develop the technologies for the future. That if you take a look at all of this from just the perspective of the US versus China alone, that's how China has surpassed us in so many ways, so many technologies from hypersonics, from their ability to gather information quicker, more effectively than we do. There are lots of areas that we need to take a more of an honest deep dive look into the future. And that's what this task force has been ultimately tasked with.",
