  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, we kicked off the task force with a hearing we heard from Senator Jim Talent as the Republican invited guests and then Michelle Florinoy who spoke as well. They were both terrific. They both set the tone for what the task force is about moving forward. We talked about attitudes and the culture at the Pentagon that sometimes limits our ability to innovate and foster the type of innovation that we need. One key takeaway from that hearing for me was Senator Talent, in his own way, talked about establishing a culture at the Pentagon of not being afraid to fail. I mean, if the Pentagon feels like they've hit it out of the ballpark every single time, that means that they're not taking a approach of risk enough, but also calling on Congress to sometimes to back off and not not hamper or hamstring the Pentagon when they do fail because we need to recognize that it's going to be the type of the level of risk that we need moving forward means that sometimes the the Pentagon needs to needs to take more risk and that was a key takeaway for me yesterday we heard from The Rand Corporation, we talked about how, in their estimation, a lot of the acquisition reforms over the past four or five years, many of them, by the way, that were brought about by Mr. Thornberry's efforts on the committee in Hask, that those have been largely effective. If the acquisitions process is working, really it's about who's developing the requirements and who's on the very front end of who is setting the process up to work with to begin with. So those are the types of issues that we've dug into so far, but we have five months to go and I hope that this will be a very productive and substantive",
