  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "It occurs to me that we have efforts, as we should, focused on the 5 to 10 year time frame. We have a national security strategy, a national defense strategy that are so important and that you're familiar with based on your service on the House Armed Service Committee. But at the same time, we're not able to do the basic blocking and tackling sometimes and passing defense budgets and appropriations and authorizations on time. And as you know better than me right now, the Department of Defense does not have We're into the new fiscal year, yet the Department of Defense doesn't have an authorization bill, and they don't have an appropriation bill. It occurs to me that people on the committees, on the House of Arms Service Committee, understand how that hurts our troops. But maybe perhaps, dare I say, some of your members who don't serve on those committees don't appreciate the damage to the same degree. If you were explaining to another member in the House of Representatives who's less focused on security issues, how would you explain the damage of a long-term continuing resolution on national security in our military?",
