  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I actually believe most of my colleagues get it. And they don't like it. They don't like the short-term CRs. It's the breakdown of the political process for both sides to come together and negotiate longer-term deals. Although, for the first time in several years, we did that last year. We set up a year-long fiscal year-long budget and funded national defense. and everyone was excited about that. We slapped each other on the back. We got it done. Now we're right back to where we were before that with another short-term CR that just passed the Senate today that I think lasts for three or four weeks and that supposedly gives us more time to negotiate a longer-term deal. We'll be back in two or three weeks and I'm not sure that anything will change. As far as convincing my colleagues, there's not a day that goes by that Secretary Esper or General Milley or someone from the Pentagon doesn't come and make that case for me or make the case that this is detrimental to readiness, it's detrimental to our ability to Getting back to China, how can we invest in longer term technologies if we're funding the government, funding the Pentagon, two or three weeks at a time or two or three months at a time, we're never going to get there with that approach. That's a sad indictment on the system, the process that we have in the Congress today and ultimately the lack of leadership to get it done.",
