  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "What some of the listeners may know is that the way Congress, both the House and Senate organizing themselves, that there's an authorization committee responsible for each of the major department or agencies in the federal government. And the House and Senate Armed Services Committees is one of the last committees that are passing an annual authorization bill on a regular basis. And thank goodness they are, but it's kind of sad that we've come to this point. And if that were to become the new norm failing to pass an authorization, I think that would not only be sad, but it would perhaps damage our national security quite tangibly. Seems to me there's maybe a moral component here. I'd be interested to know whether you think I'm overstating this a little bit. You know, when people raise the right hand like you've done to say, I want to join the military, I'm willing to put myself in harm's way, we're going to send you to go confront our enemies overseas so we don't have to confront them on our streets here, that we have a moral obligation to give people who raise the right hand everything they need to accomplish their mission and return home safely. Am I overstating that or would you agree with that?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
