    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "And some of my research lately, I've been looking at how the US can work more effectively with key allies and partners to shorten the time span that it takes from idea to fielding, make it more cost efficient to avoid capabilities gap, you reference capabilities. gaps. One example is active protection, you've served in the military, active protection systems on our army tanks. This is technology that allows our tanks to intercept incoming rockets before they hit the tank and hurt our troops. the Russians have had that for decades, right? The Israelis have had that field since 2011, yet General Milley testified earlier this year that the US defense industry as great as it is is not able yet to produce it to the degree that the Israelis have. So we're now procuring the trophy active protection system. And so that's an example how if we maintain these allies and partnerships that we have that we there can be benefit going both ways that benefits our troops. And so It seems like as we're looking at reforming the Pentagon, we also have to look at how we can work more effectively with key partners and allies.",
