  {
    "episode_id": 50746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely agree, but that can be culturally and politically difficult to do. If we're not willing to rethink how we do business, if we're not willing to maybe sacrifice some sacred cows that members of Congress protect because they have business interests or constituent interests in their district that benefit from those programs. that we found in the early days of this task force that there's a lot of interest in the industry for that very reason. Where are we going to go with this? Are we going to advocate for replacing some longstanding programs with new programs? I don't think that's really what the task force, we're not afraid to go down that path. I don't think that's really what this task force is about. It's more about creating the type of processes and mindset that will allow us to better foster innovation. There's also a key component to this and that is the workforce component. How do we foster in our education system today the type of skills and workforce ability to supply the types of skill sets across the board that it takes to to accomplish this too. So I imagine that we'll be digging into that. I recently participated in the Reagan Institute's a task force related to the innovation base that's very similar to what we're doing on the Future Defense Task Force on the Armed Services Committee. And we tried to dig into those issues, giving the finding the right approach to developing the STEM skills and the workforce to develop and foster the type of innovation base that we need.",
