    "turn_text": "Yeah, indeed. This is where I get back to taking the to developing the whole of government approach. I mean, I've introduced legislation this year that would protect sensitive research conducted on US college campuses from being infiltrated by Chinese tentacles, whether it's through Chinese related grants to colleges, or you've heard of the Confucius Institutes and the talent programs and these are snakes in the grass that are set up very intentionally by the Chinese to infiltrate and getting back to their ability to compile information and coerce Chinese students to provide them with findings of key research or even intellectual property that they are able to steal from us that's conducted on college campuses. So we can do that very easily. I mean, we shouldn't be afraid to place new protections on who is involved in research or I found through testimony of the Armed Services Committee this year that we oftentimes when the federal government funds sensitive research on a college campus, there's no mechanism to track the students who are involved in that research. It shouldn't be that way. If the Department of Defense or Department of Energy are paying for colleges or universities to conduct research, we should know who is involved in that research and where that information might flow after the research is conducted. We currently don't have the safeguards in place to do that and the Chinese have benefited from that.",
